WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.946
Hi! Can we find a rectangle's area and perimeter quickly without counting squares? Today we'll use quick methods, and work on harder shapes too.

2
00:00:12.096 --> 00:00:17.010
A rectangle is 8 cm long and 5 cm wide.

3
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.818
Area is length times breadth: 8 times 5 is 40 square centimetres.

4
00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:29.994
Perimeter is all four sides added: 8 plus 5 plus 8 plus 5 is 26 cm.

5
00:00:30.144 --> 00:00:44.010
A square has four equal sides. With 6 cm sides, the area is 6 times 6, 36 square centimetres, and the perimeter is 4 times 6, 24 cm.

6
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.210
An L-shaped figure can be cut into two rectangles.

7
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:55.578
One is 6 by 4, which is 24 square centimetres. The other is 3 by 2, which is 6.

8
00:00:55.728 --> 00:01:00.954
Add the areas: 24 plus 6 is 30 square centimetres.

9
00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:11.730
We can also work backwards: a square with a perimeter of 28 cm has sides of 28 divided by 4, which is 7 cm.

10
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:19.866
Watch out! Some pupils add just the length and breadth and get 13 cm for the perimeter. That's wrong!

11
00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:25.626
A rectangle has four sides, two lengths and two breadths, and we must add them all.

12
00:01:25.776 --> 00:01:30.858
The correct perimeter is 26 cm.

13
00:01:31.008 --> 00:01:41.922
Your turn! What is the area of a rectangle 9 cm long and 3 cm wide? Pause and think.

14
00:01:42.072 --> 00:01:48.906
9 times 3 is 27 square centimetres. Did you get it right?

15
00:01:49.056 --> 00:01:57.522
Another one! What is that rectangle's perimeter? Pause and think.

16
00:01:57.672 --> 00:02:05.274
9 plus 3 plus 9 plus 3 is 24 cm. Did you get it right?

17
00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:14.658
Here's another! What is the area of a square with 5 m sides? Pause and think.

18
00:02:14.808 --> 00:02:21.258
5 times 5 is 25 square metres. Did you get it right?

19
00:02:21.408 --> 00:02:34.530
Try one more! A rectangle has an area of 48 square centimetres and a length of 8 cm. What is its breadth? Pause and think.

20
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:44.202
8 times what is 48? 8 times 6 is 48, so the breadth is 6 cm. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:44.352 --> 00:02:55.458
Last one! A square has a perimeter of 28 cm. How long is each side? Pause and think.

22
00:02:55.608 --> 00:03:03.330
4 equal sides: 28 divided by 4 is 7 cm. Did you get it right?

23
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:18.042
Remember: a rectangle's area is length times breadth, and its perimeter is all four sides added. A square's area is side times side, and its perimeter is 4 times the side. Split harder shapes into rectangles.

24
00:03:18.192 --> 00:03:23.874
Measure your exercise book and work out its area and perimeter!

